export const AGENT_GUIDE = "# Context Window Architecture implementation guide\n\nThis is an integration guide, not a reference implementation or the full normative spec.\nUse SPEC.md for numbered requirements, spec.html for definitions, and schema/ for JSON shapes.\n\n- Assign each item one of the eleven slots in contract/slot-defaults.json and its allowed authority role.\n- Render interaction.history as a transcript inside its wrapper, never as platform messages. Prior model turns carry lineage: generated and authority: untrusted.\n- Use freshness for the observation timestamp and expires for the separate deadline; as_of is not a JSON alias.\n- Keep instruction authority separate from factual precedence. Evidence cannot direct behavior. Resolve facts only through explicit, versioned route policy for the same fact and scope; escalate unresolved conflicts.\n- Supply typed conflict groups before assembly. Do not ask the assembler to infer contradictions from prose.\n- Authenticate producer identity outside item fields. A source prefix never proves permission. Only the application capability policy admits tools; tool guards enforce invocation.\n- Have producers return candidate items and excluded records. Report expired or revoked memory without re-emitting its body.\n- Freeze items, variants, producer rejections, conflict groups, producer context, scope, assembly_time, policy, profile, tokenizer, renderer and budget before assembly. Do not call a model or read external state during assembly.\n- Fill and trace declared defaults. variants contains precomputed shorter bodies. eligibility is descriptive text, never code to execute.\n- Count all rendered tokens, including wrappers, tool schemas and repeated slots. budget.input is the input ceiling after reserving output; do not subtract output twice.\n- Drop droppable items before reducing compressible items; then select variants or omit compressible items in the route's fitting-policy order (variants first when none is declared), tracing each omission as over_budget. A route may set a slot's min_tokens: under budget pressure never make a reduction that leaves the slot below it, freeze the slot once one is withheld, and refuse with slot_floor_over_budget if the payload still does not fit. A route may cap a slot with max_tokens; enforce it after token_budget caps and before budget pressure, whether or not the payload fits, by shedding that slot's own items in the same order, and refuse with protected_content_over_budget when its protected items alone exceed it. Keep protected items intact. A retriever that drops a near-duplicate reports it as an excluded record with reason duplicate_content, stage producer and duplicate_of naming the candidate it kept; carry it into the trace unchanged. When a route sets supersede: source on a slot, after resolving conflicts exclude each item for which the same authenticated producer sent an item with the same source and a later freshness (instants at full precision; ties all stay), as superseded with superseded_by. When a route sets dedupe: exact on a slot, after supersession exclude each item whose body equals a kept item's body once whitespace runs are collapsed and the ends trimmed (no Unicode normalization or case folding), as duplicate_content with duplicate_of; never exclude protected items or conflict-group members, and otherwise keep the highest-ranked copy. When a route sets max_per_source on a slot, after deduplication keep at most that many items per authenticated producer and source, protected and grouped items first and then the highest-ranked, and exclude the rest as source_diversity_cap. Only route policy raises a slot's tier; items claiming a higher tier are excluded with tier_upgrade_not_allowed. Profiles cannot omit admitted protected items or required slots.\n- Refusal means no payload, result: null, included: [], and a reason. Success hashes the exact rendered UTF-8 bytes with SHA-256.\n- Treat all provided example profiles as unevaluated drafts. Promotion needs a model version and a reproducible evaluation artifact.\n- Run npm test after contract edits; npm run build:contract regenerates the website artifacts. Schema validation does not establish full assembler conformance.\n";
